{
  "teamId": 118,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Kansas City Royals arrived with genuine optimism after a strong offseason, and their 15-9 April record validated the excitement. Salvador Perez was an unstoppable force from the opening weeks, and Carlos Santana provided veteran presence in the middle of the lineup. The bullpen showed cracks early — Jesse Hahn surrendering big home runs in back-to-back appearances — but the offense, led by Perez and Santana, kept the Royals afloat and made April feel like a genuine contending month.\n\nMay and June told a harsher story. The team went 11-17 and 7-20 in those two months, the worst stretch of the season. Greg Holland's bullpen struggles became a recurring nightmare, headlined by Miguel Cabrera's grand slam in May. June was outright brutal — Jake Brentz gave up a Rougned Odor home run that swung nearly two-thirds of a win, and Holland surrendered another crusher to Gary Sanchez, all part of a 7-20 collapse that buried Kansas City in the standings before summer even arrived.\n\nJuly offered something of a reset. Nicky Lopez delivered a stunning walk-off-style single in early July, Salvador Perez continued his home run barrage, and Carlos Santana's big three-run shot on July 24 showed the offense was still alive. The Royals went 12-12 in July, a creditable result, even as the bullpen continued to torch leads — Scott Barlow's meltdown against Cleveland and three straight painful nights against the Indians underscored that this team's ceiling was capped by its relief corps.\n\nAugust and September settled into a dreary equilibrium. The Royals went 14-14 and 14-14 in those months, finishing 74-88 overall. There were moments of genuine joy — Whit Merrifield's grand slam, Edward Olivares's two-run homer, Nicky Lopez and Hanser Alberto delivering clutch singles against the Yankees — but they were bookended by bullpen meltdowns and starting pitching breakdowns. The season ended as it had to: a team with a transcendent Salvador Perez campaign (48 home runs, AL MVP votes) and a 4.64 ERA that simply gave away too many games to dream of October.",
